# The Fourth Dimension as a Mathematical Playground

The fourth dimension has a reputation as a haunted house of paradoxes. This notebook shows instead that it is a *playground* -- five of its most famous structures each reduce to one elementary algebraic identity. Move through the sections below, turning dials and running code as you go.

## 1. How much fits inside a four-dimensional ball?

A disc has area $\pi r^2$; a 3-ball has volume $\tfrac43\pi r^3$. The four-dimensional ball has volume $\tfrac{\pi^2}{2} r^4$ -- two copies of $\pi$, one for each independent plane of rotation. Curiously the unit-ball volume peaks not in four dimensions but in five, then decays to zero.

{{visualization:2}}

The algorithm behind this value collapses the gamma function to a factorial in even dimensions:

